#{[17592186420913 "![](images/1b/1b08e4cc9dd6ebde7f4bf544d7a76c4706dbd69a2c31d4c0fda8f816d4f871b5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x-y)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the rectangle $C$  shown above. If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186420914 "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186420915 "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [17592186420916 "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [17592186420917 "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [17592186421424 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(4-2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186421470 "![](images/95/958965484cd271c5eb7ccfc8bf913386e57b9b733719885d232e562063969ca8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [17592186421760 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=100.$"] [17592186421761 "![](images/62/62c8f6d7db345ce76bd164bb52c13e5edef9c04f18f4f07501c8694e7f64b554.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x={e}^{y}$ from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186421762 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186421763 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186421764 "![](images/95/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186421783 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2uv⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186421784 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3uv,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-3u⟩.$*"] [17592186421785 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [17592186423120 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-x-y$ for $-2\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186423121 "For a surface $S$ given by $z={x}^{2}y$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}xy+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186423122 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂y}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186423123 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-3\\le y\\le 0.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186423124 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-3y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186424097 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186424178 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$"] [17592186424179 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants."] [17592186424180 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186424181 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*"] [17592186424182 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$"] [17592186424226 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186424229 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$"] [17592186424230 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x+y)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$"] [17592186424514 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186424515 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨14xy,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186424516 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\hspace{0.222em}xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186424517 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186424518 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$"] [17592186424519 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,-1),(1,-1),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(6xy+y{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186424520 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186424521 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424522 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424523 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186424524 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424621 "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186424622 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186425876 "![](images/01/014c2310bcac5b6e9c2be2107bf8a5613b689ff29a8018d2be74fc607dd996db.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨7-2{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+10⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\sqrt{3}.$"] [17592186430947 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$"] [17592186430948 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430949 "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}y-2x,\\hspace{0.222em}x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$"] [17592186430950 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=(y+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430951 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(yz-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430952 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_